<article class="env-remark" id="047S">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/047S">Remark <span data-tag="047S">5.4.7.5</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be $\infty $-bicategories and let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a morphism of simplicial sets. If $F$ is a functor of $\infty $-bicategories and $u: X \rightarrow Y$ is an isomorphism in the $\infty $-bicategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, then $F(u): F(X) \rightarrow F(Y)$ is an isomorphism in the $\infty $-bicategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ (see Definition <a data-tag="047R" href="/tag/047R">5.4.5.12</a>). This follows by applying Remark <a data-tag="005D" href="/tag/005D">1.5.1.6</a> to the functor $\operatorname{Pith}(F): \operatorname{Pith}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Pith}(\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$ of Remark <a data-tag="01Y8" href="/tag/01Y8">5.4.7.4</a>. Beware that, if $F$ is not assumed to be a functor, then $F(u)$ need not be an isomorphism. </p>
